Argh!  My best 3.1 character ever was killed by a very annoying master lich.

Everytime I got close to killing him, he fled.  (I was on my way back to the
staircase, so that he wouldn't run as much.)  But his attacks were too swift
and fatal.  He simply cast "touch of death" until I had no more amulets of
life-saving, then "touched of death"ed me one last time.

Liches are too one-dimentional.  If you have magic resistance, they're no
big deal.  If you don't, then you could be in big trouble.

My suggestions:

+ Give the liches stronger non-magic attacks for the magic resistant folk.
  Liches should always be a little scary.

+ Change "Oh no! %s's casting the touch of death!" to "%s's casting the touch
  of death!"  "Oh no!" is rather silly.  The game does not usually empathize.

+ If the spell didn't work, it says, "Lucky for you, it didn't work!"
  Luck has nothing to do with it.  Either you were magic resistant or
  the lich rolled poorly.   If you have magic resistance, it should say, 
  "Your armor absorbs the spell."  Otherwise, it should say, "But it didn't 
  work!"

+ OK, Let's make "touch of death" luck dependent, too.  Change
        (!Antimagic && rn2(ml) > 12)
  to
        (!Antimagic && rn2(ml) > (12 + Luck))
  in the "touch of death" section of the code.  That'll help out the poor
  saps who don't have magic resistance.


What do ya all think?

